Absolute error is about as simple as physics math gets: {{viz:formula-01}}

Take the true value, subtract your measured value, and just care about the size of the gap—not whether you went too high or too low. That's why we use absolute value: being 0.5 cm short is just as much an error as being 0.5 cm over.

So in our table example: true value is 100 cm, you measured 99.5 cm. Absolute error = |100 − 99.5| = 0.5 cm. That's it—half a centimeter off, plain and simple. {{viz:anim-01}}

Notice something important though: absolute error keeps the same units as your measurement (cm, kg, seconds, whatever you're measuring). A 0.5 cm error sounds tiny for measuring a room, but it'd be huge for measuring a chip on a circuit board. That's exactly why we'll need relative and percentage error next—absolute error alone doesn't tell you if an error is a big deal or not. {{viz:3d-01}}

See how the absolute error shrinks as your measurement gets closer to the true value. {{viz:chart-01}}

Get the hang of this formula and you're halfway to understanding every other error calculation in this chapter. {{viz:text-01}} Play with real numbers here. {{viz:simulation-01}}
